      "abstract": "In the last two decades, the concept of messianism has fueled heated scholarly discussions of the philosophy of Jacques Derrida. John Caputo claims Derrida\u2019s aporetic messianism is essential to his lifelong philosophical project. Although Derrida does not believe in any specific content of messianism, Caputo argues, his writings express a desire for something we inherently cannot live to see. Caputo\u2019s approach is harshly criticized by Martin H\u00e4gguland, who reads Derrida as a \u201cradical atheist\u201d that interprets religious concepts and their messianism against themselves. According to H\u00e4gguland, when Derrida uses messianic terminology, he expresses no desire for the transcendent, but anticipates only concrete events which can happen within finite lifetimes. The debate thus captures two distinctive ways of reading Derrida \u2013 either as thinker with a desire for the transcendental or as a \u201cradical atheist.\u201d These two threads are threatening to stabilize the conversation on Derrida\u2019s writings and, more specifically, on his concept of messianism. In The Motif of the Messianic, Arthur Willemese offers an interesting possibility for thinking outside the theological-atheist dichotomy. Willemese succeeds in renewing the conversation because his primary goal is not to define Derrida\u2019s position, but to show how his conception of messianism serves as a point of departure for some of the most important discussions of philosopher Gorgio Agamben. Willemese\u2019s purpose, so he writes, is to \u201ctrace a messianic motif that connects the works of Jacques Derrida and Giorgio Agamben.\u201d He adds that \u201cfrom Agamben\u2019s more overtly messianic contemplations on metaphysics and politics, the messianic is cast backward to involve a certain interpretation of Derrida\u201d (p. xiv). Willemese\u2019s conceptualization of his book\u2019s task is thought provoking: He is careful not to claim that Agamben formulated his philosophical ideas as an answer to Derrida\u2019s messianic terminology; rather, he seeks to demonstrate how a certain reading of the Italian philosopher serves as a response to \u201cDerridean\u201d messianic thought. To a great extent, Willemese\u2019s argument is based on the definition of the messianic as a philosophical \u201cmotif,\u201d not a religious concept with a concrete history. For the sake of his study, \u201cthe messianic is, on the ontological, the political, and the linguistic level the motif of the undoing of a law, the unravelling of its fabric\u201d (p. 7). Derrida and Agamben differ, he argues, regarding how these acts of \u201cunravelling\u201d and \u201cundoing\u201d take place. Through his deconstructive methodology, Derrida reveals the aporetic structure of political, ethical, and metaphysical discourses. Nevertheless, Derrida never detaches himself from the modern philosophical dictionary and its basic assumptions. According to Willemese, Agamben \u201cholds his discourse at the threshold of deconstruction\u201d (p. 118); instead of only presenting the aporia, he attempts to think beyond its philosophical paradigms. Agamben is a thinker who is seeking the moment of infancy, a pre-discursive state in which philosophical presuppositions are temporarily suspended (p. 10). The most compelling articulations of the \u201cmessianic motif\u201d in Willemese\u2019s book are in the third and fourth chapters, which culminate in a discussion of Agamben\u2019s interpretation of Herman Melville\u2019s seminal short story \u201cBartleby, the Scrivener.\u201d Willemese explains that in",
